#pragma once
#include "common.hpp"
namespace rr
{
struct ProblemDescription {
	std::string name, title, regime, summary, setup, reference, interpretation, source;
};
inline const std::
	array<ProblemDescription, 8>
		problem_descriptions{
			{{"streaming_wave", "Streaming wave", "Exact transport",
			  "A smooth, periodic radiation wave travels at the speed of light. Its phase and amplitude test "
			  "transport accuracy.",
			  R"(<p>The initial radiation field is a sinusoidal wave in a periodic cube of side length <var>L</var>. All photons stream in one fixed direction <b>n</b>, so the physical flux is <b>F</b> = <var>cE</var><b>n</b> and the M1 pressure is <b>P</b> = <var>E</var><b>n</b><b>n</b>. There is no emission or flux damping in this test.</p>
<p>For an integer mode vector <b>m</b>, define <b>n</b> = <b>m</b>/|<b>m</b>|. The reviewed profile uses <b>m</b> = (2, −3, 1), a mean energy density of 1 and a sinusoidal amplitude of 0.5. Integer components make the phase periodic on each Cartesian face. These profile constants are defined in the solver source; the generic background/amplitude configuration entries do not set them.</p>)",
			  R"(<p>This is an exact solution of the nonlinear M1 equations in their free-streaming limit. Constant-direction transport reduces to scalar advection:</p>
<div class="equation">∂<sub>t</sub>E + c <b>n</b> · ∇E = 0</div>
<p>At a cell center <b>x</b>, the reference is the exact volume average of the translated sinusoid, including its attenuation from averaging over a finite cell:</p>
<div class="equation">Ē(<b>x</b>, t) = 1 + ½ [∏<sub>d=x,y,z</sub> sinc(π m<sub>d</sub> Δx / L)] cos[2π (<b>m</b> · <b>x</b> − c |<b>m</b>| t) / L]<br><b>F̄</b> = c Ē <b>m</b> / |<b>m</b>|</div>
<p>Here sinc(<var>a</var>) = sin(<var>a</var>)/<var>a</var>, with sinc(0) = 1. The same expression initializes the cells and supplies the comparison at the recorded final time. The phase uses the integer vector; only the flux direction is normalized.</p>)",
			  R"(<p>Look for phase error, loss of amplitude, directional bias, and the resolution dependence of all four field norms. Smooth transport can expose the nominal accuracy of the scheme, although limiting near extrema and time integration affect the measured order. Periodic boundaries and zero sources should conserve the integrated energy and each integrated flux component. A small conservation residual does not by itself establish that the wave shape is accurate.</p>)",
			  R"(<p>The benchmark is the suite's analytic periodic-advection construction, implemented by <code>streamingWave</code> in <code>octotiger/test_problems/radiation/profiles.hpp</code>. Its formula follows directly from the free-streaming M1 closure. No specific published test was identified as the source of this exact mode and amplitude.</p>)"},
			 {"streaming_front", "Streaming front", "Exact transport",
			  "A sharp, bright half-domain crosses a nearly dark background. Exact cell averages track both "
			  "moving edges through the periodic box.",
			  R"(<p>A periodic cube contains a one-dimensional square wave along <var>x</var>. Initially, the half with −<var>L</var>/2 ≤ <var>x</var> &lt; 0 has energy density <var>E</var><sub>hi</sub> = 1; the other half has <var>E</var><sub>lo</sub> = 10<sup>−10</sup>. The physical flux is (<var>cE</var>, 0, 0). The profile is uniform in <var>y</var> and <var>z</var>, and translates in the positive <var>x</var> direction at speed <var>c</var>.</p>
<p>The bright and dark levels are constants in the profile. Periodicity supplies both a rising and a falling edge. There is no emission, absorption, or flux damping in the transport test.</p>)",
			  R"(<p>The discontinuous wave is an exact nonlinear M1 free-streaming solution. The comparison integrates the translated top-hat over each cell, so a cell crossed by a front receives the appropriate bright fraction instead of a point sample.</p>
<div class="equation">a = x − ct − Δx/2, &nbsp; b = a + Δx<br>f = [I(b) − I(a)] / Δx<br>Ē = E<sub>lo</sub> + (E<sub>hi</sub> − E<sub>lo</sub>) f, &nbsp; <b>F̄</b> = (cĒ, 0, 0)</div>
<p>The periodic primitive is <var>I</var>(<var>s</var>) = <var>kL</var>/2 + min(<var>r</var>, <var>L</var>/2), where <var>k</var> = floor[(<var>s</var> + <var>L</var>/2)/<var>L</var>] and <var>r</var> = <var>s</var> + <var>L</var>/2 − <var>kL</var>. This handles any number of box crossings. The implementation clamps the computed fraction to [0, 1] to remove roundoff excursions.</p>)",
			  R"(<p>The useful diagnostics are front width, overshoot or undershoot, transport speed, and integrated conservation. Discontinuities reduce convergence order, so a second-order guide on a plot is a comparison slope rather than a pass criterion. The transverse fluxes should remain zero. Exactly zero errors have no logarithmic convergence order and are reported with a dash.</p>)",
			  R"(<p>The suite constructs this periodic half-filled square wave directly in <code>streamingFront</code> and <code>frontIntegral</code> in <code>octotiger/test_problems/radiation/profiles.hpp</code>. It is an exact-advection verification problem; the available source does not attribute this particular setup to a published benchmark.</p>)"},
			 {"gaussian_pulse", "Gaussian pulse", "Telegraph equation",
			  "A small Gaussian perturbation evolves in a fixed scattering medium. A Fourier solution of the "
			  "telegraph equation supplies the reference.",
			  R"(<p>The initial state is a smooth Gaussian energy excess of amplitude <var>A</var> and width <var>w</var> over a uniform background <var>E</var><sub>bg</sub>, with zero flux. The Gaussian is periodized on the three-dimensional cube. A prescribed, stationary scattering medium damps flux at rate <var>cχ</var>, while there is no energy source. Gas motion, recoil, and heating are absent from this radiation-only setup.</p>
<div class="equation">E(<b>x</b>, 0) = E<sub>bg</sub> + A ∑<sub><b>j</b>∈ℤ³</sub> exp[−|<b>x</b> + L<b>j</b>|² / w²], &nbsp; <b>F</b>(<b>x</b>, 0) = 0</div>
<p>The standard CGS templates give <var>E</var><sub>bg</sub> = 1 erg/cm³, <var>A</var> = 0.01 erg/cm³, <var>w</var> = 6 × 10⁹ cm, and <var>χ</var> = 1.6666666667 × 10<sup>−10</sup> cm<sup>−1</sup>. The saved settings below take precedence for a particular run.</p>)",
			  R"(<p><strong>The reference solves the telegraph equation.</strong> Linearizing the moment equations about an isotropic radiation background gives <b>P</b> = <var>E</var><b>I</b>/3 and</p>
<div class="equation">∂<sub>t</sub>E + ∇ · <b>F</b> = 0<br>∂<sub>t</sub><b>F</b> + (c²/3) ∇E = −cχ <b>F</b><br>∂<sub>t</sub>²E + cχ ∂<sub>t</sub>E = (c²/3) ∇²E</div>
<p>For each periodic Fourier wavevector <b>k</b> = 2π<b>j</b>/<var>L</var>, the generator solves the damped oscillator analytically. Define α = <var>cχ</var>/2, λ² = α² − <var>c</var>²|<b>k</b>|²/3, and the mode propagators</p>
<div class="equation">B<sub>k</sub>(t) = e<sup>−αt</sup> sinh(λt)/λ<br>A<sub>k</sub>(t) = e<sup>−αt</sup> cosh(λt) + α B<sub>k</sub>(t)<br>δÊ(<b>k</b>, t) = A<sub>k</sub>(t) δÊ(<b>k</b>, 0)<br><b>F̂</b>(<b>k</b>, t) = −i(c²/3) <b>k</b> B<sub>k</sub>(t) δÊ(<b>k</b>, 0)</div>
<p>Negative λ² uses the equivalent real sine/cosine expressions; λ = 0 uses <var>B</var> = <var>t</var>e<sup>−αt</sup>. The zero mode keeps the mean energy constant. Stable exponential expressions in <code>telegraphMode</code> avoid cancellation and overflow. Flux relaxation and propagation speed <var>c</var>/√3 are retained.</p>
<p>The C++ generator first forms Gaussian cell averages using error-function integrals, with image shifts −4 through +4 on each axis. It transforms these values with FFTW, removes every Nyquist plane on the even grid so odd spectral derivatives remain real, and evolves each retained mode. Both initial and final arrays use this same filter. The inverse transform is divided by <var>N</var>³ once, and the uniform background is added to the energy. The resulting <code>OTRAD001</code> binary stores all four physical fields at the two times.</p>)",
			  R"(<p>The numerical solver evolves nonlinear M1; the telegraph solution is its small-perturbation, isotropic-pressure reference. At finite amplitude, measured error includes a closure-model difference as well as spatial and temporal discretization. Refinement can therefore reach a model-error floor. Initial spectral truncation also depends on resolution. The prescribed source damps flux at rate <var>cχ</var>; its numerical integration depends on the recorded method, as described below.</p>
<p>In the periodic domain total energy should remain constant. Flux damping belongs in the signed source budget when assessing flux-integral conservation.</p>)",
			  R"(<p>The suite derives its reference from the linearized two-moment equations above. The implementation is <code>telegraphMode</code> in <code>profiles.hpp</code>, the <code>ReferenceData</code> reader/writer in <code>reference.hpp</code>, and <code>radiation_results/cpp/gen_radiation_reference.cpp</code>. The generator was recovered from the September 13 source patch and adapted to the September 17 interfaces while retaining its Fourier construction.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.fftw.org/fftw-paper-ieee.pdf">Frigo &amp; Johnson (2005), “The Design and Implementation of FFTW3”</a>, describes the transform library. The Gaussian and its telegraph reference are the suite's construction; FFTW supplies the discrete transforms.</p>)"},
			 {"equilibrium_sphere",
			  "Equilibrium sphere", "Steady diffusion", "A smooth spherical emitter balances outward radiation transport. An error-function solution describes its steady diffusion equilibrium.",
			  R"(<p>A Gaussian light source is centered in a stationary medium of prescribed opacity <var>χ</var>. It emits total luminosity ℒ over all space, with a smooth width <var>w</var>:</p>
<div class="equation">q(r) = ℒ exp(−r²/w²) / (π<sup>3/2</sup> w³), &nbsp; r = |<b>x</b>|</div>
<p>The domain starts from the steady diffusion solution on top of background <var>E</var><sub>bg</sub>. Boundaries are nonperiodic: ghost cells are fixed to cell averages of that same analytic equilibrium. The incoming background is part of this boundary state. The gas remains fixed; the source adds radiation energy and the medium damps flux.</p>
<p>The standard CGS templates use <var>w</var> = 6 × 10⁹ cm, <var>χ</var> = 1.6666666667 × 10<sup>−10</sup> cm<sup>−1</sup>, <var>E</var><sub>bg</sub> = 1 erg/cm³, and ℒ = 2.698132122 × 10²⁹ erg/s. This is a smooth Gaussian emitter. The saved settings below give the actual values used.</p>)",
			  R"(<p>In steady isotropic diffusion, <var>D</var> = <var>c</var>/(3<var>χ</var>), ∇ · <b>F</b> = <var>q</var>, and <b>F</b> = −<var>D</var>∇<var>E</var>. Integrating the enclosed Gaussian luminosity gives</p>
<div class="equation">E(r) = E<sub>bg</sub> + [3χℒ/(4πc)] erf(r/w)/r<br><b>F</b>(<b>x</b>) = [ℒ/(4πr³)] [erf(s) − 2s e<sup>−s²</sup>/√π] <b>x</b>, &nbsp; s = r/w</div>
<p>The apparent singularities are removable: <var>E</var>(0) = <var>E</var><sub>bg</sub> + 3<var>χ</var>ℒ/(2π<sup>3/2</sup><var>cw</var>) and <b>F</b>(0) = 0. The implementation uses series expansions near the origin.</p>
<p>Initial, boundary, and comparison states are cell averages evaluated with four-point Gauss–Legendre quadrature on each axis (64 samples per cell). The smooth-field averages therefore have quadrature error. The Gaussian source has an exact cell integral, evaluated as a product of three error-function differences:</p>
<div class="equation">q̄ = (ℒ/Δx³) ∏<sub>d=x,y,z</sub> ½ {erf[(x<sub>d</sub> + Δx/2)/w] − erf[(x<sub>d</sub> − Δx/2)/w]}</div>
<p>The prescribed source adds Δ<var>t q̄</var> to energy and damps flux at rate <var>cχ</var>. Its numerical integration depends on the recorded method, as described below. The steady reference itself is independent of time.</p>)",
			  R"(<p>This is a diffusion-limit equilibrium test. Nonlinear M1 has an anisotropic pressure at finite flux, so the diffusion solution need not remain exactly stationary in that model. Errors can reflect closure differences, transport/source splitting, discretization, and quadrature. The plots measure equilibrium drift and Cartesian directional effects as resolution changes.</p>
<p>Energy injected inside the finite cube should balance its outward transport and the change in stored energy. ℒ is the all-space luminosity; use the measured in-domain source budget rather than assuming the finite cube receives exactly ℒ. Symmetry can make net flux integrals very small, which is why their conservation normalization includes an energy-based scale.</p>)",
			  R"(<p>This smooth Gaussian-emitter test is defined by <code>equilibriumSphere</code>, <code>sphereAverage</code>, and <code>bulbSourceAverage</code> in <code>octotiger/test_problems/radiation/profiles.hpp</code>. Its reference follows by solving the steady diffusion equation for that source. The available implementation does not identify it as a verbatim published sphere benchmark, so the provenance here is the suite's analytic construction.</p>)"},
             {"subcritical_shock", "Subcritical radiative shock", "Coupled radiation hydrodynamics",
              "Radiation heats gas ahead of a shock, forming a precursor whose gas temperature remains below the downstream equilibrium temperature.",
              R"(<p>This test evolves gas and radiation together. The intended subcritical regime has a radiative precursor with T<sub>gas,pre</sub> &lt; T<sub>downstream</sub>; radiation and gas temperatures need not agree in the precursor or at the shock spike.</p>
<p>Ensman's original calculation used a moving spherical piston at 6 × 10<sup>5</sup> cm/s in a shell spanning 8 × 10<sup>11</sup> to 8.7 × 10<sup>11</sup> cm. Initially ρ = 7.78 × 10<sup>−10</sup> g/cm³, the absorption coefficient is 3.115 × 10<sup>−10</sup> cm<sup>−1</sup>, and the 300 equal-mass zones begin at 10 K at the outer edge, rising by 0.25 K per zone inward. The gas uses γ = 5/3 and fully ionized hydrogen (mean molecular weight μ = 0.5).</p>
<p><strong>Local Cartesian adaptation.</strong> The box spans −3.5 × 10<sup>10</sup> to +3.5 × 10<sup>10</sup> cm. Gas moves toward a reflecting left wall at v<sub>x</sub> = −6 × 10<sup>5</sup> cm/s, with fixed inflow at the right face and zero-gradient transverse boundaries. Temperature varies linearly from 85 K at the wall to 10 K at the inflow. Absorption follows χ = 3.115 × 10<sup>−10</sup>(ρ / 7.78 × 10<sup>−10</sup> g/cm³) cm<sup>−1</sup>. The default duration is 38300 s. A stationary planar wall replaces the spherical moving piston; the M1 moment closure replaces the original transfer treatment.</p>)",
              R"(<p>No exact analytic profile is supplied for the coupled problem. Ensman (1994), Figure 9 at t = 3.83 × 10<sup>4</sup> s, is an original numerical comparison target. Its curves are not embedded here, digitized, or used to manufacture error norms.</p>
<div class="note"><h3>Approximate analytic shock scales</h3><p>Let U be the imposed wall-frame inflow speed and R = k<sub>B</sub>/(μm<sub>H</sub>) = 1.65022 × 10<sup>8</sup> erg/(g K). A strong, gas-pressure-dominated <strong>adiabatic</strong> shock with negligible upstream pressure has</p><div class="equation">ρ<sub>2</sub>/ρ<sub>1</sub> = 4, &nbsp; u<sub>1</sub> = 4U/3, &nbsp; s = U/3<br>T<sub>2</sub> ≈ 2(γ − 1)u<sub>1</sub>²/[R(γ + 1)²] = U²/(3R)</div><p>Here u<sub>1</sub> is the upstream speed relative to the shock and s is the outward shock speed in the wall frame. For U = 6 km/s, this gives s ≈ 2 km/s and T<sub>2</sub> ≈ 727 K. These are approximate conservation-law scales, not an analytic transient profile or an error threshold. Upstream heating, finite Mach number, and radiative energy losses modify the jump.</p></div>)",
              R"(<p>Inspect the radiation precursor, the difference between gas and radiation temperatures, the gas temperature overshoot, and the shock speed. A subcritical shock has gas immediately upstream cooler than the downstream equilibrium gas. The physical regime must be assessed after sufficient evolution; the case name alone does not demonstrate that the regime has been reached.</p>)",
              R"(<p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1086/173889">Ensman (1994), “Test Problems for Radiation and Radiation-Hydrodynamics Codes,” ApJ 424, 275</a>, §§5.1–5.2, pp. 283–285, describes the original radiative shocks. <a href="https://articles.adsabs.harvard.edu/pdf/1994ApJ...424..275E">Original paper PDF</a>. The original spherical geometry, piston boundary, and time must be matched before claiming quantitative agreement with its figures.</p>)"},
             {"supercritical_shock", "Supercritical radiative shock", "Coupled radiation hydrodynamics",
              "A faster shock produces a stronger radiative precursor. Gas ahead of the shock approaches the downstream equilibrium temperature.",
              R"(<p>The supercritical case couples hydrodynamics, absorption, emission, and radiation momentum exchange. In a developed supercritical shock the gas immediately upstream is heated approximately to the downstream equilibrium temperature, while a narrow gas temperature spike remains at the front.</p>
<p>Ensman's original shell, density, opacity, initial temperature gradient, and ideal-gas parameters match the subcritical calculation. The piston speed is increased to 2 × 10<sup>6</sup> cm/s. The local Cartesian adaptation uses the same box, temperature gradient, density-dependent absorption, reflecting left wall, right inflow, and transverse boundaries as the local subcritical case, with v<sub>x</sub> = −2 × 10<sup>6</sup> cm/s and default duration 7500 s. A planar wall and M1 moment closure replace the original moving spherical piston and transfer treatment.</p>)",
              R"(<p>No exact analytic solution is supplied. Ensman (1994), Figure 13 at t = 7.5 × 10<sup>3</sup> s, provides a numerical comparison target. Figure 10 also shows evolution at 860, 4000, 7500, and 13000 s. Differences of geometry, equation of state, opacity, or boundaries change those profiles.</p>
<div class="note"><h3>Approximate analytic shock scales</h3><p>For γ = 5/3, μ = 0.5, and imposed wall-frame speed U, a strong, gas-pressure-dominated <strong>adiabatic</strong> baseline gives ρ<sub>2</sub>/ρ<sub>1</sub> = 4, shock-relative upstream speed u<sub>1</sub> = 4U/3, wall-frame shock speed s = U/3, and T<sub>2</sub> ≈ U²/(3R), where R = k<sub>B</sub>/(μm<sub>H</sub>) = 1.65022 × 10<sup>8</sup> erg/(g K).</p><p>For U = 20 km/s the baseline is s ≈ 6.67 km/s and T<sub>2</sub> ≈ 8080 K. <strong>8080 K is not the predicted plateau of the cooled radiative shock.</strong> Radiative losses can increase compression above four and change both speed and plateau temperature; Ensman's radiating calculation has a plateau near 4000 K in Figure 15, versus about 8000 K for its adiabatic comparison.</p><p>In the approximate steady supercritical, gas-pressure-dominated diffusion picture, T<sub>−</sub> ≈ T<sub>2</sub> and T<sub>+</sub> ≈ (3 − γ)T<sub>2</sub> = 4T<sub>2</sub>/3. T<sub>−</sub> and T<sub>+</sub> are the gas temperatures immediately ahead of and at the spike; these relations use the <em>cooled downstream plateau</em>, not the adiabatic estimate. They are qualitative checks, not exact M1 reference curves or pass/fail tolerances.</p></div>)",
              R"(<p>Look for precursor heating to the downstream equilibrium temperature, the narrow gas spike, and the asymmetric radiation flux. Ensman's developed solution includes negative radiation flux behind the shock. Refine space and time to distinguish a resolved precursor and spike from numerical smearing; a short smoke run cannot demonstrate these late-time features.</p>)",
              R"(<p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1086/173889">Ensman (1994), ApJ 424, 275</a>, §5.2, Figures 10–15; <a href="https://articles.adsabs.harvard.edu/pdf/1994ApJ...424..275E">original paper PDF</a>. The subcritical and supercritical labels describe physical regimes, rather than universal velocity thresholds independent of the surrounding medium.</p>)"},
             {"radiating_blast", "Radiating blast wave", "Coupled radiation hydrodynamics",
              "A spherical energy release drives a shock and radiation precursor into a radial atmosphere, evolved on a Cartesian mesh.",
              R"(<p>The blast problem evolves an initially localized energy deposition with coupled gas and radiation. Its useful diagnostics include shock position, gas and radiation temperatures, density, and outward radiation flux.</p>
<p>Ensman's original problem is a spherical shell from 10<sup>12</sup> to 10<sup>13</sup> cm with ρ(r) = 10<sup>−6</sup>(r/10<sup>12</sup> cm)<sup>−7</sup> g/cm³. The shell is initially in radiative equilibrium with luminosity 10<sup>34</sup> erg/s. An energy of 10<sup>47</sup> erg is deposited in the innermost zone, followed by zero inner luminosity and velocity. The opacity uses the electron-scattering coefficient as pure absorption. This stratified spherical setup is distinct from a uniform-medium Cartesian thermal bomb.</p>
<p><strong>Spherical blast on a Cartesian mesh.</strong> The initial density, gas and radiation temperatures, radiation flux, and Gaussian energy deposition depend only on radius. The computational box spans ±10<sup>13</sup> cm and contains a filled core of radius R<sub>0</sub> = 10<sup>12</sup> cm. Density is 10<sup>−6</sup>[max(r,R<sub>0</sub>)/R<sub>0</sub>]<sup>−7</sup> g/cm³. The 10<sup>47</sup> erg gas-energy deposition is a Gaussian of width R<sub>0</sub>. Exact error-function cell integrals and finite-box normalization preserve its total at every uniform-grid resolution. The initial radiation field uses an LTE diffusion envelope based on luminosity 10<sup>34</sup> erg/s and outer E = L/(4πcR<sub>out</sub>²), with a regularized core. A streaming floor E ≥ (1 + 10<sup>−12</sup>)|F|/c keeps the approximate atmosphere realizable while preserving its radial flux. The central luminosity is removed when evolution starts. The default duration is 22000 s.</p><p>The filled core, Gaussian deposition width, Cartesian grid, M1 closure, streaming floor, and approximate LTE diffusion initialization are deliberate adaptations. They differ from the original hollow shell, innermost Lagrangian-zone deposition, and radiation-transfer relaxation.</p>)",
              R"(<p>No analytic radiative blast profile is supplied. Ensman (1994), Figures 17–22, follows the original numerical solution from energy deposition through radiative breakout and later shell formation.</p>
<div class="note"><h3>What the Sedov–Taylor limit can tell us</h3><p>For an energy-conserving point explosion into a cold, uniform medium with negligible radiative losses, the spherical similarity solution gives</p><div class="equation">R<sub>shock</sub>(t) = ξ(γ) [E<sub>0</sub>t²/ρ<sub>0</sub>]<sup>1/5</sup>, &nbsp; v<sub>shock</sub> = 2R<sub>shock</sub>/(5t)</div><p>This is a useful separate adiabatic benchmark. It is not an analytic solution of the present radiating blast: the finite Gaussian deposition, filled core, steep r<sup>−7</sup> envelope, initial radiation field, and escaping luminosity introduce different scales and energy transport. Substituting the envelope exponent into a simple power-law Sedov formula does not provide a valid reference for this truncated atmosphere. Quantitative validation here requires conservation and resolution studies plus an independent radiation-hydrodynamics solution with the same setup.</p></div>)",
              R"(<p>Track the shock and radiation precursor separately, together with energy exchange and boundary losses. In Ensman's original calculation the precursor breaks out near 5800 s and the luminosity peaks near 9600 s. These times depend on that paper's shell and boundary conditions; they are not pass/fail thresholds for an adapted setup.</p>)",
              R"(<p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1086/173889">Ensman (1994), ApJ 424, 275</a>, §5.3, pp. 286–290, Figures 17–22; <a href="https://articles.adsabs.harvard.edu/pdf/1994ApJ...424..275E">original paper PDF</a>. Published profiles are numerical evidence, not an analytic reference. The local runs retain their actual numerical parameters and elapsed physical time.</p>)"},
             {"marshak", "Marshak wave", "Nonequilibrium radiation diffusion",
              "Boundary illumination heats initially cold stationary material. Radiation and material energy profiles are compared with the semi-analytic Marshak diffusion solution.",
              R"(<p>The normalized cube spans −1 ≤ x,y,z ≤ 1. The material density and momentum are fixed; radiation exchanges energy with the material through a linear equilibrium law E<sub>eq</sub> = e. The propagation speed is c = 1, absorption and extinction are both χ = 100, and the default final time is t = 1. Equal material and radiation energy capacities correspond to ε = 1. Gravity and hydrodynamic transport are disabled.</p><p>This is a heating test in normalized units, rather than an ideal-gas temperature benchmark. Plots show E/4 and e/4. The saved metadata and source record the actual boundary treatment and reference quadrature.</p>)",
              R"(<p>The Su–Olson reference is the solution of a linear, gray, nonequilibrium <strong>diffusion</strong> problem in a semi-infinite stationary medium:</p><div class="equation">∂<sub>t</sub>E − (1/3χ)∂<sub>x</sub>²E = χ(e − E)<br>∂<sub>t</sub>e = χ(E − e)</div><p>With ξ = √3χ(x + 1), τ = χt, u = E/4, and v = e/4, the reference has u − (2/√3)∂<sub>ξ</sub>u = 1 at the illuminated boundary and zero initial u and v. The existing integral quadrature supplies radiation energy, material energy, and diffusion flux. Reference values are evaluated at cell centers; the exported comparison does not apply an additional cell-volume average.</p>)",
              R"(<p>Inspect the penetration depth and delay between radiation and material heating. The full-volume L1, L2, and maximum norms include material energy as well as all four radiation moments. Nonlinear M1 has finite propagation speed and a closure different from diffusion near the illuminated boundary and early front. Consequently these norms include model and boundary differences; they are not an exact-M1 order test. A zero transverse-flux error has no logarithmic convergence order. The numerical boundary combines a prescribed incoming half-isotropic bath with outgoing moments from the interior M1 intensity. Its finite-grid ghost-cell/HLL realization and early causal transient can differ from the diffusion boundary solution.</p>)",
              R"(<p><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022407396845249">Su &amp; Olson (1996), “Benchmark results for the non-equilibrium Marshak diffusion problem,” JQSRT 56, 337–351</a>, gives the nonequilibrium diffusion benchmark. The local reference is implemented in <code>src/test_problems/marshak/marshak.cpp</code>; the run records source hashes for reproducibility.</p>)"}}};
inline ProblemDescription fld_problem_description(ProblemDescription d)
{
    if (!fld_case(d.name)) {
        d.summary = "Unsupported by the stationary FLD backend. " + d.summary;
        d.reference = "<p class=\"note\">This problem is available for M1 RK1 only in the current runner. No FLD result is claimed.</p>" + d.reference;
        return d;
    }
    d.regime = "Stationary flux-limited diffusion";
    if (d.name == "streaming_wave" || d.name == "streaming_front") {
        const bool wave = d.name == "streaming_wave";
        d.regime = "Vacuum closure comparison";
        d.summary = wave ?
            "The same periodic sinusoidal energy used by the M1 wave evolves with gradient-driven FLD flux. FLD has no independently prescribed beam direction." :
            "The same periodic bright half-domain used by the M1 front evolves with gradient-driven FLD flux. Its two edges do not represent one directed beam.";
        d.setup = wave ?
            R"(<p>The periodic cube starts with the exact cell averages of E = 1 + ½ cos(2π<b>m</b>·<b>x</b>/L), with <b>m</b> = (2, −3, 1). The domain, initial energy, zero extinction, and absence of emission match the M1 streaming-wave problem. Gas dynamics and gravity are disabled.</p>)" :
            R"(<p>The periodic cube starts with E = 1 in the left half and E = 10<sup>−10</sup> in the right half, using exact cell averages at the edges. The field is uniform in the transverse directions. The domain, initial energy, zero extinction, and absence of emission match the M1 streaming-front problem. Gas dynamics and gravity are disabled.</p>)";
        d.setup += R"(<p>M1 initializes a directed streaming flux. FLD instead derives its flux from the energy gradient. At zero extinction and nonzero gradient, the Levermore–Pomraning vacuum limit is <b>F</b> = −cE ∇E/|∇E|. The implemented flat-gradient convention is <b>F</b> = 0. A spatially varying periodic field therefore has oppositely directed fluxes on opposite slopes; it cannot preserve M1's single beam direction.</p>)";
        d.reference = R"(<p><strong>No analytic spatial solution of this nonlinear vacuum FLD problem is supplied.</strong> The translated M1 sinusoid or top-hat is an exact solution of directed M1 transport, not of the FLD closure. A constant-diffusion heat solution would change the zero-extinction problem and is not substituted. Accordingly, FLD exports numerical profiles without analytic-reference or error columns.</p><p>An exact integral reference follows from the energy equation and periodic boundaries:</p><div class="equation">d/dt ∫<sub>V</sub>E dV = −∮<sub>∂V</sub><b>F</b>·d<b>A</b> = 0</div>)";
        d.reference += wave ?
            R"(<p>The exact initial and conserved mean is ⟨E⟩ = 1, so the total radiation energy is L³.</p>)" :
            R"(<p>The exact initial and conserved mean is ⟨E⟩ = (1 + 10<sup>−10</sup>)/2, so the total radiation energy is L³(1 + 10<sup>−10</sup>)/2.</p>)";
        d.interpretation = R"(<p>The report compares the complete recorded energy history with that exact integral and checks E ≥ 0 and |F| ≤ cE on the exported slice. These necessary checks do not validate the spatial profile. Resolution and timestep studies measure numerical sensitivity; paired M1/FLD curves measure closure differences, not analytic errors. FLD does not evolve or conserve independent flux moments, so M1 momentum-ledger tests do not apply.</p>)";
        d.source = R"(<p>The shared initial energy comes from <code>streamingWave</code> or <code>streamingFront</code> in <code>octotiger/test_problems/radiation/profiles.hpp</code>. The vacuum flux follows directly from the large-R limit λ(R)R → 1 of the implemented limiter. The integrated-energy reference follows from the divergence theorem and periodic boundaries.</p>)";
    } else if (d.name == "equilibrium_sphere") {
        d.summary = "A prescribed Gaussian emitter drives FLD in a stationary scattering medium, compared with its analytic steady linear-diffusion equilibrium.";
        d.setup = R"(<p>The source is q(r) = ℒ exp(−r²/w²)/(π<sup>3/2</sup>w³), with constant positive Rosseland extinction χ and zero Planck absorption. The material stays fixed and receives no recoil or heating. The source deposits its exact cell-integrated radiation energy during each accepted timestep.</p><p>The initial radiation energy is a cell average of the steady linear-diffusion equilibrium. On the finite cube, each boundary uses Dirichlet energy sampled from the same equilibrium, averaged over the boundary face by four-point Gauss–Legendre quadrature in each tangential direction. FLD derives its own initial and evolved flux from its nonlinear limiter; no independent flux is prescribed. The recorded parameters give the source luminosity, width, background, and opacity.</p>)";
        d.reference = R"(<p><strong>Analytic diffusion-limit reference; not an exact solution of nonlinear FLD.</strong> For D₀ = c/(3χ), the stationary equations ∇·<b>F</b><sub>ref</sub> = q and <b>F</b><sub>ref</sub> = −D₀∇E<sub>ref</sub> give</p><div class="equation">E<sub>ref</sub>(r) = E<sub>bg</sub> + [3χℒ/(4πc)] erf(r/w)/r<br><b>F</b><sub>ref</sub>(<b>x</b>) = [ℒ/(4πr³)] [erf(s) − 2s exp(−s²)/√π] <b>x</b>, &nbsp; s = r/w</div><p>The expressions have finite limits at the origin, with zero flux there. Initial and comparison cell averages use four-point Gauss–Legendre quadrature on each axis. The reference is time independent and solves the linear diffusion problem with the prescribed source and boundary energy.</p><p>Nonlinear FLD uses λ(R) rather than 1/3, so finite-gradient limiter effects can move the numerical state away from this linear equilibrium. The reference flux is −D₀∇E<sub>ref</sub>, not the nonlinear limiter applied to E<sub>ref</sub>. Reported slice differences include that model mismatch, spatial and temporal error, boundary discretization, and quadrature error.</p>)";
        d.interpretation = R"(<p>Inspect equilibrium drift, radial symmetry, positivity, the derived flux bound, and the energy budget. The finite cube intercepts only a fraction of the Gaussian's all-space luminosity ℒ: the integrated source is ℒ[erf(L/(2w))]³. The change in stored energy plus outward boundary transport must match this in-domain injection. The imposed equilibrium boundary can exchange energy with the domain; it is not a vacuum boundary. Slice differences do not imply an exact nonlinear-FLD convergence order.</p>)";
        d.source = R"(<p>The analytic Gaussian-emitter solution, its volume quadrature, and its exact source cell integral are implemented by <code>equilibriumSphere</code>, <code>sphereAverage</code>, and <code>bulbSourceAverage</code> in <code>octotiger/test_problems/radiation/profiles.hpp</code>. The construction follows by integrating the enclosed spherical source luminosity and then the linear diffusion gradient.</p>)";
    } else if (d.name == "gaussian_pulse") {
        d.summary = "A periodized Gaussian energy perturbation diffuses in a prescribed stationary scattering medium. Flux is derived from the energy gradient.";
        d.setup = R"(<p>The Gaussian energy and fixed material state use the same initial energy cell averages as the M1 case. The prescribed scattering opacity is constant, the boundaries are periodic, and there is no radiation energy source, gas motion, or material heating. M1 starts with zero evolved flux; FLD instead derives its initial flux from the initial energy gradient, so the models do not share an independent flux initial condition.</p><p>The saved run settings specify the Gaussian background, amplitude, width, opacity, domain, and actual final time. The Fourier generator stores the initial energy and the analytic linear diffusion evolution, with reference flux derived from that reference energy.</p>)";
        d.reference = R"(<p><strong>Analytic diffusion-limit reference; not an exact solution of nonlinear FLD.</strong> The FLD reference solves the periodic constant-coefficient heat equation, rather than the M1 telegraph equation:</p>
<div class="equation">∂<sub>t</sub>E = D₀∇²E, &nbsp; D₀ = c/(3χ)<br>δÊ(<b>k</b>,t) = δÊ(<b>k</b>,0) exp(−D₀|<b>k</b>|²t)<br><b>F̂</b><sub>ref</sub> = −iD₀<b>k</b>δÊ(<b>k</b>,t)</div>
<p>The generator transforms the same periodized Gaussian cell averages used to initialize the simulation and removes the same Nyquist planes. Each retained mode evolves analytically; the constant background is unchanged. Reference flux is the analytic gradient of those modes, including at the initial time. Thus the reference is exact for linear diffusion of the represented, filtered initial energy, up to floating-point evaluation.</p>
<p>The actual solver uses D = cλ(R)/χ, where R = |∇E|/(χE) and λ(R) = (2+R)/(6+3R+R²). Only as R → 0 does λ → 1/3. Reference differences at finite R include this model difference as well as spatial and temporal errors. The reported L1, L2, and maximum differences cover the exported projected slice, not the full three-dimensional volume.</p>)";
        d.interpretation = R"(<p>Inspect energy spreading, positivity, the bound |F| ≤ cE, and periodic energy conservation. Compare resolutions and timesteps separately. A paired M1/FLD curve difference includes a difference between physical closures and transient flux relaxation, not only discretization error. Initial spectral filtering also depends on resolution.</p>)";
        d.source = R"(<p>The periodic heat-equation Fourier solution above is implemented by <code>radiation_results/cpp/gen_radiation_reference.cpp --model fld</code>. The generator shares initial energy construction with M1, but evolves each mode by diffusion instead of the M1 telegraph propagator. The <code>ReferenceData</code> reader uses the resulting cell-average arrays.</p><p><a href="https://www.fftw.org/fftw-paper-ieee.pdf">Frigo &amp; Johnson (2005), “The Design and Implementation of FFTW3”</a>, describes the transform library used for the Fourier construction.</p>)";
    } else if (d.name == "marshak") {
        d.summary = "An incoming radiation bath heats initially cold, stationary material through coupled nonlinear FLD and material energy exchange.";
        d.setup = R"(<p>The cube spans −1 to +1 in normalized units, with c = 1, Planck and Rosseland extinction 100, and equal radiation/material energy capacities. Initially E = 0 and material energy is 10<sup>−20</sup>. The material density and velocity remain fixed while its thermal energy evolves.</p><p>The left boundary supplies an incoming hemisphere flux of 1 through the FLD Robin condition cE/2 + D ∂E/∂n = 2. The right boundary is vacuum, cE/2 + D ∂E/∂n = 0; transverse faces reflect. M1 uses a moment boundary construction, so the two discrete boundary operators are not identical.</p>)";
        d.reference = R"(<p><strong>Analytic diffusion-limit reference; not an exact solution of nonlinear FLD.</strong> The Su–Olson solution supplies radiation energy, material energy, and the flux derived from its energy gradient for a semi-infinite linear diffusion problem:</p>
<div class="equation">∂<sub>t</sub>E − [1/(3χ)]∂<sub>x</sub>²E = χ(e−E), &nbsp; ∂<sub>t</sub>e = χ(E−e)<br>ξ = √3χ(x+1), &nbsp; τ = χt, &nbsp; E<sub>ref</sub> = 4u(ξ,τ), &nbsp; e<sub>ref</sub> = 4v(ξ,τ)<br>F<sub>x,ref</sub> = −(4/√3)∂u/∂ξ, &nbsp; F<sub>y,ref</sub> = F<sub>z,ref</sub> = 0</div>
<p>Here c = 1, χ = 100, and the equal-capacity parameter is ε = 1. The left Marshak condition is u − (2/√3)∂u/∂ξ = 1. Integral representations evaluate the analytic solution numerically. Exported reference values are sampled at cell centers; numerical fields are cell averages, adding a sampling difference.</p>
<p>The FLD calculation instead has a nonlinear flux limiter and a finite domain with a vacuum boundary on the right. These differences matter near the early front and when radiation reaches that boundary. Slice discrepancy norms therefore combine model, boundary, quadrature, sampling, and discretization differences; they are not an exact-FLD convergence test.</p>)";
        d.interpretation = R"(<p>Inspect radiation and material heating, penetration depth, nonnegative energies, the derived flux bound, and energy change after accounting for boundary transport and material exchange. Finite-domain and limiter effects matter when interpreting model differences. Short paired runs exercise the implementation and reporting pipeline; they do not establish late-time benchmark accuracy.</p>)";
    }
    d.source += R"(<p><a href="https://adsabs.harvard.edu/pdf/1981ApJ...248..321L">Levermore &amp; Pomraning (1981), “A flux-limited diffusion theory,” ApJ 248, 321–334</a>, derives flux-limited diffusion and its classical diffusion limit. Saved reference metadata records whether an analytic profile is supplied and distinguishes its equations from the nonlinear limiter evolved by this solver.</p>)";
    return d;
}
} // namespace rr
